Hands-on Lab – Implementation Using AWS CLI
---------------------------------------------
We will use the existing variables:
echo $BUCKET
echo $DIST_ID


- $BUCKET – Existing S3 bucket in ap-south-1.
- $DIST_ID – Existing CloudFront distribution ID.


------------

Step 1: Create an IAM role for Lambda
Why? Lambda needs an IAM execution role to write logs to CloudWatch and invalidate objects in our CloudFront distribution.

Get the AWS account ID:
#ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)


Create the trust policy:

#cat <<'EOF' > trust-policy.json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Principal": {"Service": "lambda.amazonaws.com"},
    "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
  }]
}
EOF


Create the role:

#aws iam create-role \
  --role-name training-event-lambda-role \
  --assume-role-policy-document file://trust-policy.json


Attach CloudWatch logging permissions:

# aws iam attach-role-policy \
  --role-name training-event-lambda-role \
  --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2: Grant Lambda permission to invalidate CloudFront
Why? By default, Lambda cannot perform CloudFront operations. We must explicitly grant the cloudfront:CreateInvalidation permission.

Create the policy:
#cat <<EOF > cloudfront-policy.json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": "cloudfront:CreateInvalidation",
    "Resource": "arn:aws:cloudfront::$ACCOUNT_ID:distribution/$DIST_ID"
  }]
}
EOF


Attach the policy:
#aws iam put-role-policy \
  --role-name training-event-lambda-role \
  --policy-name CloudFrontInvalidationPolicy \
  --policy-document file://cloudfront-policy.json

---------------------------------
Step 3: Create the Lambda function
Why? Lambda contains the business logic that processes the S3 upload event and requests CloudFront cache invalidation.

Create the Python program:

#cat <<'EOF' > lambda_function.py
import boto3
import os
import uuid
from urllib.parse import unquote_plus, quote

cloudfront = boto3.client('cloudfront')

def lambda_handler(event, context):

    distribution_id = os.environ['DISTRIBUTION_ID']
    paths = set()

    for record in event.get('Records', []):
        key = unquote_plus(record['s3']['object']['key'])

        path = '/' + quote(key, safe='/-_.~')
        paths.add(path)

        # The root URL also serves index.html
        if key == 'index.html':
            paths.add('/')

    if not paths:
        return {'message': 'No objects to invalidate'}

    response = cloudfront.create_invalidation(
        DistributionId=distribution_id,
        InvalidationBatch={
            'Paths': {
                'Quantity': len(paths),
                'Items': sorted(paths)
            },
            'CallerReference': str(uuid.uuid4())
        }
    )

    return {
        'status': 'success',
        'invalidation_id': response['Invalidation']['Id'],
        'paths': sorted(paths)
    }
EOF


Package the function:
#zip function.zip lambda_function.py


Create the Lambda function:
#ROLE_ARN=$(aws iam get-role \
  --role-name training-event-lambda-role \
  --query 'Role.Arn' --output text)

sleep 10

aws lambda create-function \
  --function-name training-s3-cloudfront-invalidation \
  --runtime python3.13 \
  --role "$ROLE_ARN" \
  --handler lambda_function.lambda_handler \
  --zip-file fileb://function.zip \
  --timeout 30 \
  --memory-size 128 \
  --environment "Variables={DISTRIBUTION_ID=$DIST_ID}" \
  --region ap-south-1

---------------------------------------------------

Step 4: Allow S3 to invoke Lambda
Why? S3 must be authorized to invoke our Lambda function whenever an object is uploaded.

#LAMBDA_ARN=$(aws lambda get-function \
  --function-name training-s3-cloudfront-invalidation \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --query 'Configuration.FunctionArn' \
  --output text)


Grant invocation permission:
#aws lambda add-permission \
  --function-name training-s3-cloudfront-invalidation \
  --statement-id AllowS3Invocation \
  --action lambda:InvokeFunction \
  --principal s3.amazonaws.com \
  --source-arn arn:aws:s3:::$BUCKET \
  --source-account $ACCOUNT_ID \
  --region ap-south-1

------------------------------------------------------

Step 5: Configure S3 Event Notification
Why? This is the actual event-driven integration. We configure S3 to generate an event whenever a new object is created or an existing object is overwritten.


#cat <<EOF > notification.json
{
  "LambdaFunctionConfigurations": [{
    "Id": "S3UploadTrigger",
    "LambdaFunctionArn": "$LAMBDA_ARN",
    "Events": ["s3:ObjectCreated:*"]
  }]
}
EOF


Apply the notification configuration:
#aws s3api put-bucket-notification-configuration \
  --bucket "$BUCKET" \
  --notification-configuration file://notification.json \
  --region ap-south-1

